### How do you write a custom functional interface?

Declare an interface with one abstract method and annotate it.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

@FunctionalInterface
public interface SalaryCheck {
    boolean test(Employee e, double threshold);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CustomFIDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SalaryCheck earnsMoreThan = (e, t) -> e.getSalaryAsDouble() > t;

        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
            new Employee("Rajesh", "Loans", 75000.0),
            new Employee("Priya",  "IT",   82000.0),
            new Employee("Amit",   "IT",   45000.0)
        );

        List<Employee> highEarners = employees.stream()
            .filter(e -> earnsMoreThan.test(e, 50_000))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        highEarners.forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.getName()));
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh
Priya
```

### Why not use BiPredicate<Employee, Double>?

| Feature | SalaryCheck | BiPredicate<Employee, Double> |
|---|---|---|
| Name | Domain-specific | Generic |
| Primitive boxing | None (primitive double) | Autoboxes double to Double |
| Readability | SalaryCheck.test(e, 50000) | BiPredicate.test(e, 50000.0) |

A named custom interface documents intent and avoids the boxing overhead of Double.

### What does @FunctionalInterface enforce?

```java
// CORRECT: exactly one abstract method
@FunctionalInterface
interface SalaryCheck {
    boolean test(Employee e, double threshold);
}

// WRONG: two abstract methods → compile error
@FunctionalInterface
interface BrokenCheck {
    boolean test(Employee e, double threshold);
    String describe();  // second abstract method!
}
```

Default and static methods are fine; they do not count as abstract.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface SalaryCheck {
    boolean test(Employee e, double threshold);

    default SalaryCheck negate() {
        return (e, t) -> !this.test(e, t);
    }
}
```

### When should you create a custom interface vs reuse built-ins?

| Create custom when | Reuse built-in when |
|---|---|
| The interface needs a domain-specific name | A standard name like Predicate suffices |
| Primitive parameters avoid boxing overhead | All parameters are objects |
| The interface needs default/static methods | No extra methods needed |

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: omitting the annotation and accidentally adding a second abstract method later.

```java
// Without @FunctionalInterface, someone could add a second method
// and break all existing lambdas. The annotation prevents this.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- @FunctionalInterface is a safety net, not a requirement. Lambdas work on any single-abstract-method interface.
- Custom interfaces shine for domain DSLs: SalaryCheck, TransferValidator, LoanEligibility.
- Keep the standard set (Predicate, Function, Consumer, Supplier) as the default choice.
- Adding default methods to a custom functional interface provides composition (negate, and, or) without breaking the single-method contract.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Is @FunctionalInterface required for a lambda?" No, but it enforces the contract.
- "Can a functional interface have default methods?" Yes, as many as needed.
- "Can a functional interface extend another interface?" Yes, as long as the total has exactly one abstract method.
